# Track 4 — E2E round 3: 40 vCPU total budget
#
# Rebalanced (2026-06) in stages after docker stats + sweep analysis:
#   1. token-issuer (RS256 signing) was the bottleneck, validators idle.
#   2. proof-queue Redis then pinned (status-poll reads) -> own larger cap.
#   3. Sweeps showed throughput tracks token-issuer REPLICA count, not vCPU
#      (R1/R2 @ 2 issuers ~210; R3 @ 4 issuers ~424). The issuer is
#      HTTP-concurrency-bound per process, so it scales by replicas, not
#      by CPU. Bumped issuer 4 -> 6 (matching R4) to let the 30 verifiers
#      become the bottleneck instead. Funded from the idle validators.
#
# | Service              | cpus x replicas | subtotal |
# |----------------------|-----------------|----------|
# | proof-queue Redis    | 0.7 x 1         | 0.7      |
# | snark+token Redis    | 0.15 x 2        | 0.3      |
# | request-handler      | 0.65 x 4        | 2.6      |
# | verifier-selector    | 0.6 x 1         | 0.6      |
# | token-issuer         | 0.8 x 6         | 4.8      |
# | snark-verifier       | 1.0 x 30        | 30.0     |
# | token-validator (x5) | 0.2 x 5         | 1.0      |
# | TOTAL                |                 | 40.0     |
#
# Expectation after this: throughput should climb from ~424 toward ~600-660
# (the 30-verifier ceiling) and the cost column should start rising toward
# ~1.4 as the weighted selector finally spills into the expensive verifiers.
# If cost still stays ~1.1, the jam moved to the handler or proof-queue.
#
# Load with:  set -a; source e2e-throughput-round3.env; set +a
PROOF_CPUS=0.7
REDIS_CPUS=0.15
HANDLER_REPLICAS=4
HANDLER_CPUS=0.65
SELECTOR_CPUS=0.6
ISSUER_REPLICAS=6
ISSUER_CPUS=0.8
SNARK_REPLICAS=30
SNARK_COUNT=30
# Applied manually on each App EC2: docker update --cpus=$VALIDATOR_CPUS ...
VALIDATOR_CPUS=0.2
